# Generated by packages/natives `bun run gen:native` (platform tarball; rebuild locally / in release)
packages/natives/native/embedded-addons.*.tar.gz

# napi-rs's isolated build directory: the .node artifacts and the generated
# index.d.ts land here before they are copied into place. Never committed, and
# saying so here is also what tells the doc-path gate that a doc naming it is
# describing a build output rather than pointing at a file that went missing.
packages/natives/native/.build/

# Local agent scratch: Claude Code worktrees and per-machine config, never part of the repo
.claude/

# get.veyyon.dev deploy tree (build artifact from website/build.mjs)
/website-get/

# Bun install cache, written into the repo root by some tool invocations.
relative-cache/

# Bun temp spill directory (`bun/@t@/*.pile`), written into the repo root by some
# tool invocations for the same reason as the cache above.
bun/

# A project-scope secret vault, written by `/secret add --scope project` into the project's
# own `.veyyon/`. It is an encrypted credential store and must never be committed: the
# ciphertext plus its binding is not something to publish, and a clone of a repo carrying one
# cannot decrypt it anyway, which currently breaks `/secret` for whoever cloned it. The
# `.unreadable-*` siblings are what `/secret discard` renames a broken vault to, and they hold
# the same sealed entries.
# Anchored with `**/` deliberately: a pattern containing a slash is relative to this file, so
# a bare `.veyyon/vault.json` would ignore only a vault at the repo root and miss the one a
# session started in a subdirectory writes, which is where the observed debris landed.
**/.veyyon/vault.json
**/.veyyon/vault.json.unreadable-*

# Perf-lane scratch: captured boot frames, A/B run directories and raw version probes, written
# by the performance lane into the repo root. Two reasons it must not be committed. It is
# regenerated debris that means nothing to anyone else, and the captured frames embed ABSOLUTE
# paths from the machine that took them, so committing one publishes the operator's home
# directory and username in a stack trace.
/.perf-lane/

# The test sandbox's guest artifacts: a kernel image and a ~390MB initramfs, built
# by `bash scripts/test-sandbox/run.sh --build` from the local docker image. They are
# large, machine-local, and reproducible from scripts/test-sandbox/guest/Dockerfile plus
# the bun version, so there is nothing to gain from committing them. `boot-verified` is
# a marker written only after the guest has actually booted and mounted the repo;
# it must not travel to a machine that has not run that check.
/scripts/test-sandbox/guest/.build/

# Raw provider traffic captured by VEYYON_REQ_DEBUG=1. It lands in the working directory,
# which is usually a repo, and it is the request as it went on the wire: prompts, file
# contents, and whatever a provider echoed back. Credential headers are redacted before the
# file is written and the file is created 0600, but it is still not something to commit.
rr-session-*.json
rr-session-*.res.log

# Agent session state written into the repo. A veyyon session started from a working
# directory persists its transcript beside the code it was working on, under an
# agent directory whose name is generated per session, so the paths are unpredictable
# and never the same twice. One of these was committed by accident and then dirtied
# every worktree that checked it out, because the agent that owned it kept appending.
# The transcript is the conversation verbatim: prompts, file contents, tool output.
*/sessions/*.jsonl
scratch-docs-lane/

# The release-notes staging buffer. `scripts/ci-release-notes.ts` writes it from
# the CHANGELOG on a tag build and the release job feeds it straight to the
# GitHub API, so CI regenerates it every time and nothing reads a committed copy.
# It was tracked as an empty placeholder, which bought nothing and left a real
# release body one `git add` away from landing in the repository.
/release-notes.md
/release-notes-dryrun.md

